# RYA-690 §3D — kill the recurring merge conflict at its source.
#
# SEQUENCE.md is an append-only narrative log: every ticket inserts one line
# directly under the current date heading, i.e. at an identical anchor. Two
# concurrent branches therefore collide there by construction, whatever those
# tickets did — three conflicts of exactly this shape occurred in one batch
# (PR #188, PR #193 twice). `union` makes git concatenate both sides instead of
# asking, and the conflict class disappears.
#
# CAVEAT, stated rather than discovered later: if two branches each CREATE a new
# date heading, union keeps both copies of that heading. That is cosmetic and
# trivially deduped, and far cheaper than conflicting on every merge. Entry order
# within a day becomes merge order rather than authored order, which is fine for a
# narrative log where the date is the unit of meaning.
SEQUENCE.md merge=union

# CODEX_STATE_REGISTER.md deliberately gets NO union merge.
#
# This is the whole point of RYA-690. "Accept both" is CORRECT for the append-only
# log above and WRONG for the register's `**Version:` header, which is single-valued
# — union there would have AUTOMATED the eight-header bug rather than prevented it.
# Same gesture, opposite correctness. The register is protected structurally instead:
# the header carries no narrative (so there is nothing to merge but one short token),
# and `scripts/check_register_freshness.py --structure-only` asserts there is exactly
# one header line and that it names the newest changelog row.
